   |\n   108\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   109\t| 6  | UDP channel: `dao.stream.udp` with fragmentation keyed by  | The toy over UDP with a 48 KiB value (inside the default     | clj cljs cljd |\n   110\t|    | attachment, address, direction and id; adapters            | 64 KiB maximum); loss injection recovers by resend;          |               |\n   180\tMoved from the spec's section 8, which keeps only the normative summary.\n   181\tEvery existing network path, verified against the tree, and its fate:\n   182\t\n   183\t- `dao.stream.ws` and the jvm, node, browser, dart adapters: **subsumed**.\n   184\t  Already a channel: writer plus deposit medium plus the spec's 3.1\n   185\t  projection. Its accept and disclaim frames and served-path table become\n   186\t  unnecessary once `not-found` is an answer per identity; retiring them is\n   187\t  slice 3.\n   188\t- `dao.stream.serving`: **retired**. Forwards a source into every accepted\n   189\t  socket and flattens a source `gap` into a detach: the copy model. Its\n   190\t  consumers are `yin.repl.serve` and `dao.jing.remote` (which requires and\n   191\t  drives it); both are retired by slices 4 and 5.\n   192\t- `dao.stream.forward`: **unrelated**. A local copy step; replication by\n   193\t  convention, not exposure.\n   194\t- `dao.stream.rpc`: **convention-over**. Correlation by id over two streams\n   195\t  is the spec's section 5 service; its state machine may be kept as one\n   196\t  such vocabulary over reflections, or retired in slice 5.\n   197\t- `dao.stream.rpc.ws`: **retired**. Its only job is translating `:ws/`\n   198\t  envelopes for rpc; the 3.1 projection is the channel binding now.\n   199\t- `dao.stream.apply` as a wire envelope: **retired**. Its ok-or-error\n   200\t  wrapper duplicates the outcome map. The `:dao.stream.apply/call` AST node\n   201\t  and the VM's FFI bridge keep the name and are unrelated to the network.\n   202\t- `yin.repl.serve`, `connect`, `driver`, `adapter`, the `daostream:ws://`\n   203\t  URL: **convention-over**. An eval service: `requests` and `answers`\n   204\t  streams, `:op/eval` as payload; the URL becomes a remote descriptor for\n   205\t  that pair. The shared-shell privilege is its interpreter's policy.\n     1\t(ns dao.stream.rpc\n     2\t  \"Transport-neutral, polling RPC state machines for DaoStream v2.\n     3\t\n     4\t   This layer deliberately owns no socket, scheduler, atom, promise, callback,\n     5\t   retry policy, or transport registry.  A composition supplies two explicit\n     6\t   handles (a request writer and a response reader), owns the returned state,\n     7\t   and chooses when to step `request!`, `poll!`, and `serve-once!`.\"\n     8\t  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n     9\t            [dao.stream.apply :as apply]))\n    10\t\n    11\t\n    12\t;; =============================================================================\n    13\t;; Public data vocabulary\n    14\t;; =============================================================================\n    15\t\n    16\t(def max-safe-id\n    17\t  \"Largest integer that remains exactly representable by every supported host.\"\n    18\t  9007199254740991)\n    19\t\n    20\t\n    21\t(def event-key :dao.stream.rpc/event)\n    22\t(def response-key :dao.stream.rpc/response)\n    23\t(def lifecycle-key :dao.stream.rpc/lifecycle)\n    24\t(def diagnostic-key :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic)\n    25\t(def code-key :dao.stream.rpc/code)\n    26\t\n    27\t\n    28\t(def lifecycle-values\n    29\t  \"The lifecycle vocabulary belongs to dao.stream.apply.  Transports decode\n    30\t   their local events into these values before the RPC core sees them.\"\n    31\t  #{:dao.stream.apply/established\n    32\t    :dao.stream.apply/detached\n    33\t    :dao.stream.apply/ended\n    34\t    :dao.stream.apply/not-found\n    35\t    :dao.stream.apply/transport-error\n    36\t    :dao.stream.apply/diagnostic})\n    37\t\n    38\t\n    39\t(defn response-event\n    40\t  [response]\n    41\t  {event-key :dao.stream.rpc/response response-key response})\n    42\t\n    43\t\n    44\t(defn lifecycle-event\n    45\t  [lifecycle]\n    46\t  {event-key :dao.stream.rpc/lifecycle lifecycle-key lifecycle})\n    47\t\n    48\t\n    49\t(defn diagnostic-event\n    50\t  ([code]\n    51\t   (diagnostic-event code nil))\n    52\t  ([code value]\n    53\t   {event-key :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic\n    54\t    diagnostic-key {code-key code\n    55\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/value value}}))\n    56\t\n    57\t\n    58\t(defn ignore-event\n    59\t  []\n    60\t  {event-key :dao.stream.rpc/ignore})\n    61\t\n    62\t\n    63\t(defn safe-id?\n    64\t  \"True for an integer which round-trips without precision loss across CLJ,\n    65\t   CLJS, and CLJD.  IDs are routing tokens, not application values.\"\n    66\t  [id]\n    67\t  (and (integer? id) (<= 0 id max-safe-id)))\n    68\t\n    69\t\n    70\t;; =============================================================================\n    71\t;; Explicit client state\n    72\t;; =============================================================================\n    73\t\n    74\t(defn client-state\n    75\t  \"Create caller-owned RPC client state.\n    76\t\n    77\t   `writer` is the request path. `reader` and `response-cursor` are the\n    78\t   independent response path.  `:decode`, when present, transforms one raw\n    79\t   response-medium value into an RPC event made by this namespace; nil means a\n    80\t   bare apply response stream.  No cursor is fabricated here.\"\n    81\t  ([writer reader response-cursor]\n    82\t   (client-state writer reader response-cursor {}))\n    83\t  ([writer reader response-cursor {:keys [me decode]}]\n    84\t   {:writer writer\n    85\t    :reader reader\n    86\t    :cursor response-cursor\n    87\t    :me me\n    88\t    :decode decode\n    89\t    :next-id 0\n    90\t    :outstanding {}\n    91\t    :unsent nil\n    92\t    :completed []\n    93\t    :diagnostics []\n    94\t    :terminal nil}))\n    95\t\n    96\t\n    97\t(def init-client client-state)\n    98\t\n    99\t\n   100\t(defn server-state\n   101\t  \"The server state is owned by dao.stream.apply, which owns the envelope.\"\n   102\t  [request-cursor]\n   103\t  (apply/server-state request-cursor))\n   104\t\n   105\t\n   106\t(defn serve-once!\n   107\t  \"Advance one server request/response step.  This is an aliasing boundary, not\n   108\t   a second server implementation; apply remains the sole envelope owner.\"\n   109\t  [handlers request-handle response-handle state]\n   110\t  (apply/serve-once! handlers request-handle response-handle state))\n   111\t\n   112\t\n   113\t(defn- rpc-result\n   114\t  [outcome state & {:as extra}]\n   115\t  (merge {:dao.stream.rpc/outcome outcome\n   116\t          :dao.stream.rpc/state state}\n   117\t         extra))\n   118\t\n   119\t\n   120\t(defn- valid-operation-result\n   121\t  [op result]\n   122\t  (if (stream/valid-outcome? op result)\n   123\t    result\n   124\t    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n   125\t     :dao.stream/error :dao.stream/invalid-operation-result}))\n   126\t\n   127\t\n   128\t(defn- completion\n   129\t  [request & {:keys [response reason]}]\n   130\t  (cond-> {:dao.stream.rpc/id (:id request)\n   131\t           :dao.stream.rpc/op (:op request)\n   132\t           :dao.stream.rpc/args (:args request)}\n   133\t    response (assoc :dao.stream.rpc/response response)\n   134\t    reason (assoc :dao.stream.rpc/reason reason)))\n   135\t\n   136\t\n   137\t(defn- append-completion\n   138\t  [state request & {:as fields}]\n   139\t  (update state :completed conj (apply completion request (mapcat identity fields))))\n   140\t\n   141\t\n   142\t(defn- append-diagnostic\n   143\t  [state code value]\n   144\t  (update state :diagnostics conj {code-key code\n   145\t                                   :dao.stream.rpc/value value}))\n   285\t     (-> state\n   286\t         (assoc :unsent nil)\n   287\t         (append-completion request :reason reason))\n   288\t     state)))\n   289\t\n   290\t\n   291\t;; =============================================================================\n   292\t;; Response decoding and correlation\n   293\t;; =============================================================================\n   294\t\n   295\t(defn- normalize-event\n   296\t  [value]\n   297\t  (cond\n   298\t    (nil? value) (ignore-event)\n   299\t    (apply/response? value) (response-event value)\n   300\t    (contains? lifecycle-values value) (lifecycle-event value)\n   301\t    (and (map? value) (keyword? (get value event-key))) value\n   302\t    :else (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/malformed-response value)))\n   303\t\n   304\t\n   305\t(defn- decode-value\n   306\t  [state value]\n   307\t  (try\n   308\t    (normalize-event (if-let [decode (:decode state)] (decode value) value))\n   309\t    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n   310\t      (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/decode-error value))))\n   311\t\n   312\t\n   313\t(defn- lose-outstanding\n   314\t  [state reason terminal?]\n   315\t  (let [state (reduce (fn [s [id request]]\n   316\t                        (append-completion s (assoc request :id id) :reason reason))\n   317\t                      state\n   318\t                      (:outstanding state))]\n   319\t    (cond-> (assoc state :outstanding {})\n   320\t      terminal? (assoc :terminal reason))))\n   321\t\n   322\t\n   323\t(defn handle-event\n   324\t  \"Apply one decoded RPC event to explicit client state.\n   325\t\n   326\t   Transport adapters use `response-event`, `lifecycle-event`,\n   327\t   `diagnostic-event`, and `ignore-event`; raw bare apply responses are also\n   328\t   accepted.  This function has no cursor operation and is useful for testing\n   329\t   adapters independently from a response medium.\"\n   330\t  [state event]\n   331\t  (let [event (normalize-event event)\n   332\t        kind (get event event-key)]\n   333\t    (cond\n   334\t      ;; A terminal binding cannot complete an old request later.  Keep the\n   335\t      ;; event as diagnostic data rather than silently treating it as a reply.\n   336\t      (:terminal state)\n   337\t      (let [state (append-diagnostic state :dao.stream.rpc/event-after-terminal event)]\n   338\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic state\n   339\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state))))\n   340\t\n   341\t      (= kind :dao.stream.rpc/ignore)\n   342\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/ignored state)\n   343\t\n   344\t      (= kind :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic)\n   345\t      (let [diagnostic (get event diagnostic-key)\n   346\t            state (append-diagnostic state (get diagnostic code-key) diagnostic)]\n   347\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic state\n   348\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/diagnostic (last (:diagnostics state))))\n   349\t\n   350\t      (= kind :dao.stream.rpc/response)\n   351\t      (let [response (get event response-key)]\n   352\t        (if-not (apply/response? response)\n   353\t          (handle-event state (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/malformed-response response))\n   354\t          (let [id (apply/response-id response)]\n   355\t            (if-let [request (get (:outstanding state) id)]\n   356\t              (let [state (-> state\n   357\t                              (update :outstanding dissoc id)\n   358\t                              (append-completion (assoc request :id id)\n   359\t                                                 :response response))]\n   360\t                (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/responded state\n   361\t                            :dao.stream.rpc/id id\n   362\t                            :dao.stream.rpc/response response))\n   363\t              (handle-event state\n   364\t                            (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/unsolicited-response response))))))\n   365\t\n   366\t      (= kind :dao.stream.rpc/lifecycle)\n   367\t      (let [lifecycle (get event lifecycle-key)]\n   368\t        (case lifecycle\n   369\t          :dao.stream.apply/established\n   370\t          (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/established state)\n   371\t\n   372\t          :dao.stream.apply/diagnostic\n   373\t          (handle-event state (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/transport-diagnostic event))\n   374\t\n   375\t          :dao.stream.apply/detached\n   376\t          (let [state (lose-outstanding state lifecycle true)]\n   377\t            (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/lost state :dao.stream.rpc/reason lifecycle))\n   378\t\n   379\t          (:dao.stream.apply/ended\n   380\t            :dao.stream.apply/not-found\n   381\t            :dao.stream.apply/transport-error)\n   382\t          (let [state (lose-outstanding state lifecycle true)]\n   383\t            (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/lost state :dao.stream.rpc/reason lifecycle))\n   384\t\n   385\t          (handle-event state (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/unhandled-event event))))\n   386\t\n   387\t      :else\n   388\t      (handle-event state (diagnostic-event :dao.stream.rpc/unhandled-event event)))))\n   389\t\n   390\t\n   391\t(defn- poll-read\n   392\t  [state]\n   393\t  (let [read-result (valid-operation-result :next (stream/next (:reader state) (:cursor state)))\n   394\t        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome read-result)]\n   395\t    (case outcome\n   396\t      :dao.stream/ok\n   397\t      ;; Advance before decoding. A malformed or unsolicited element is thereby\n   398\t      ;; consumed exactly once and cannot poison polling.\n   399\t      (handle-event (assoc state :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor read-result))\n   400\t                    (decode-value state (:dao.stream/value read-result)))\n   401\t\n   402\t      :dao.stream/blocked\n   403\t      (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/idle state :dao.stream.rpc/read read-result)\n   404\t\n   405\t      :dao.stream/gap\n   406\t      (let [state (-> state\n   407\t                      (assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor read-result))\n   408\t                      (lose-outstanding :dao.stream/gap false))]\n   409\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/lost state\n   410\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason :dao.stream/gap\n   411\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/read read-result))\n   412\t\n   413\t      (:dao.stream/end :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch :dao.stream/invalid-cursor\n   414\t                       :dao.stream/transport-error)\n   415\t      (let [state (lose-outstanding state outcome true)]\n   416\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/lost state\n   417\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason outcome\n   418\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/read read-result))\n   419\t\n   420\t      (let [state (lose-outstanding state :dao.stream/transport-error true)]\n   421\t        (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/lost state\n   422\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/reason :dao.stream/transport-error\n   423\t                    :dao.stream.rpc/read read-result)))))\n   424\t\n   425\t\n   426\t(defn poll!\n   427\t  \"Read at most `budget` response-medium elements.\n   428\t\n   429\t   A `:dao.stream/blocked` result yields immediately; this function never loops\n   430\t   waiting for a response.  Each successful read installs the exact successor\n   431\t   cursor returned by that handle before decoding the element.\"\n   432\t  ([state] (poll! state 1))\n   433\t  ([state budget]\n   434\t   (cond\n   435\t     (:terminal state) (rpc-result :dao.stream.rpc/terminal state)\n     1\t(ns yin.repl.serve\n     2\t  \"The server side of the DaoStream Yin REPL — Phase R4.\n     3\t\n     4\t   `serve!` composes an endpoint and returns immediately with one explicit\n     5\t   value: the service-lifetime `/repl` stream created at start (D3), the\n     6\t   boundary control medium, the bounded acceptance handoff pool, the\n     7\t   composition-owned lifecycle medium with its already-minted cursor, and the\n     8\t   one shared shell every session evaluates against (D4).  Binding a listener is\n     9\t   host policy, injected through `yin.repl.host`; every fact about it —\n    10\t   `:bind-succeeded`, `:bind-failed`, `:upgrade-failed`, `:listener-error`,\n    11\t   `:stopped` — arrives as plain data on the lifecycle medium, and no host error\n    12\t   object crosses the boundary.\n    13\t\n    14\t   `step` is the single server driver: it observes lifecycle, calls the\n    15\t   transport's `endpoint-step` through `dao.stream.serving`, adopts accepted\n    16\t   sessions, and advances each one against a single serially threaded REPL\n    17\t   state.  It never loops on `blocked`, never waits, and never schedules\n    18\t   itself.\n    19\t\n    20\t   Each session's request medium is observed through a `dao.stream.waitset`\n    21\t   probe: a non-advancing `:next` entry whose wake selects the session for\n    22\t   its own `session-step` re-read.  A session holds one active waiter —\n    23\t   while a response append is pending, the retry belongs to the host's\n    24\t   cadence and no probe is parked.\"\n    25\t  (:require [dao.data :as data]\n    26\t            [dao.stream :as stream]\n    27\t            [dao.stream.apply :as apply]\n    28\t            [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ring]\n    29\t            [dao.stream.rpc.ws :as rpc-ws]\n    30\t            [dao.stream.serving :as serving]\n    31\t            [dao.stream.waitset :as waitset]\n    32\t            [dao.stream.ws :as ws]\n    33\t            [yin.repl.connect :as connect]\n    34\t            [yin.repl :as repl]\n    35\t            [yin.repl.host.common :as host-common]))\n    36\t\n    37\t\n    38\t;; =============================================================================\n    39\t;; Composition constants\n    40\t;; =============================================================================\n    41\t\n    42\t(def service-capacity\n    43\t  \"The `/repl` service-lifetime stream carries no ordinary outbound value in\n    44\t   this slice, so capacity 1 cannot evict in a correct composition.  It exists\n    45\t   to anchor the served identity and to make `stop!` observable as `:ws/ended`.\"\n    46\t  1)\n    47\t\n    48\t\n    49\t(def control-capacity 1024)\n    50\t(def request-capacity 8192)\n    51\t(def lifecycle-capacity 256)\n    52\t(def default-slot-count 8)\n    53\t(def default-bind-host \"127.0.0.1\")\n    54\t(def lifecycle-budget 64)\n    55\t\n    56\t\n    57\t(def ^:private diagnostic-bounds\n    58\t  \"Bound on operator-facing diagnostic text built from unbounded internal\n    59\t   state (unvalidated bind config, host-reported lifecycle values).\"\n    60\t  {:depth 3 :items 8 :chars 200})\n    61\t\n    62\t\n    63\t(def eval-operation :op/eval)\n    64\t\n    65\t\n    66\t(def incomplete-input-code\n    67\t  \"This server's refusal of a well-formed request whose source is an unbalanced\n    68\t   form.  The envelope owner's malformed-request code would misreport it as a\n    69\t   codec defect.\"\n    70\t  :yin.repl.serve/incomplete-input)\n    71\t\n    72\t\n    73\t(def wildcard-hosts #{\"0.0.0.0\" \"::\" \"[::]\" \"*\"})\n    74\t\n    75\t\n    76\t(def event-key :yin.repl.endpoint/event)\n    77\t(def value-key :yin.repl.endpoint/value)\n    78\t\n    79\t\n    80\t(def event-kinds\n    81\t  \"The fixed lifecycle event set.  An unknown kind is surfaced as a diagnostic\n    82\t   rather than silently ignored or trusted.\"\n    83\t  #{:bind-succeeded :bind-failed :upgrade-failed :listener-error :stopped})\n    84\t\n    85\t\n    86\t(def outbox-event-key :yin.repl.serve/event)\n    87\t(def text-key :yin.repl.serve/text)\n    88\t\n    89\t\n    90\t(def portable-admission\n    91\t  {:retention :evict-oldest :capacity control-capacity :value-domain :portable-values})\n    92\t\n    93\t\n    94\t(def request-admission\n    95\t  {:retention :evict-oldest :capacity request-capacity :value-domain :portable-values})\n    96\t\n    97\t\n    98\t(def handoff-admission\n    99\t  {:retention :evict-oldest :capacity 1 :value-domain :host-values})\n   100\t\n   101\t\n   102\t;; =============================================================================\n   103\t;; Small composition helpers\n   104\t;; =============================================================================\n   105\t\n   106\t(defn- buffer\n   107\t  [capacity]\n   108\t  (:dao.stream/handle\n   109\t    (ring/create! {:dao.stream/type ring/transport-type\n   110\t                   ring/capacity-key capacity})))\n     1\t(ns yin.repl.connect\n     2\t  \"The client side of the DaoStream Yin REPL — Phase R3.\n     3\t\n     4\t   One connection is one explicit value.  `open` canonicalizes the URL to the\n     5\t   request-target form `dao.stream.ws.md` defines, creates the boundary's single\n     6\t   capacity-8192 traffic medium, mints both of its cursors, composes the\n     7\t   WebSocket boundary, and only then calls `attach!`, receiving a handle at\n     8\t   once.  No promise, future, callback, atom, clock, or namespace global is\n     9\t   created here, and no open event can race ahead of a cursor.\n    10\t\n    11\t   The traffic medium is reused across reconnects: `disconnect` is `close!`,\n    12\t   and reattaching is `attach!` plus `rebind`, which keeps the response cursor\n    13\t   and takes the new attachment id.  WebSocket spellings stay confined to the\n    14\t   R1 decoder in `dao.stream.rpc.ws`; everything observed here is the\n    15\t   neutral `:dao.stream.apply/…` lifecycle vocabulary.\"\n    16\t  (:require [clojure.string :as str]\n    17\t            [dao.data :as data]\n    18\t            [dao.stream :as stream]\n    19\t            [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ring]\n    20\t            [dao.stream.rpc :as rpc]\n    21\t            [dao.stream.rpc.ws :as rpc-ws]\n    22\t            [dao.stream.ws :as ws]\n    23\t            [yin.repl.host.common :as host-common]))\n    24\t\n    25\t\n    26\t;; =============================================================================\n    27\t;; Composition constants\n    28\t;; =============================================================================\n    29\t\n    30\t(def traffic-capacity\n    31\t  \"Declared capacity of the boundary's one traffic medium, in elements.  It is\n    32\t   reused across reconnects because a client boundary has at most one active\n    33\t   attachment at a time.\"\n    34\t  8192)\n    35\t\n    36\t\n    37\t(def traffic-admission\n    38\t  {:retention :evict-oldest\n    39\t   :capacity traffic-capacity\n    40\t   :value-domain :portable-values})\n    41\t\n    42\t\n    43\t(def lifecycle-budget\n    44\t  \"Maximum lifecycle elements one observation reads, so a noisy medium cannot\n    45\t   make a single step unbounded.\"\n    46\t  32)\n    47\t\n    48\t\n    49\t(def url-prefix \"daostream:\")\n    50\t(def ws-scheme \"ws://\")\n    51\t(def secure-scheme \"wss://\")\n    52\t(def default-port 8080)\n    53\t\n    54\t\n    55\t(def repl-path\n    56\t  \"The path an empty URL path means for this REPL (D3).  An explicit `/`\n    57\t   remains `/`; only an absent path becomes the served REPL stream.\"\n    58\t  \"/repl\")\n    59\t\n    60\t\n    61\t(def service-identity\n    62\t  \"The logical identity of the stream a REPL endpoint serves.  A descriptor\n    63\t   names a served stream, so a client that only typed a URL still needs one;\n    64\t   a stable service name keeps `attach!` honest across endpoint restarts.\"\n    65\t  \"yin.repl/repl\")\n    66\t\n    67\t\n    68\t(def ^:private diagnostic-bounds\n    69\t  \"Bound on operator-facing diagnostic text built from unbounded internal\n    70\t   state (raw connect URLs).\"\n    71\t  {:depth 3 :items 8 :chars 200})\n    72\t\n    73\t\n    74\t(def outcome-key :yin.repl.connect/outcome)\n    75\t(def connection-key :yin.repl.connect/connection)\n    76\t(def client-key :yin.repl.connect/client)\n    77\t(def descriptor-key :yin.repl.connect/descriptor)\n    78\t(def message-key :yin.repl.connect/message)\n    79\t(def event-key :yin.repl.connect/event)\n    80\t(def text-key :yin.repl.connect/text)\n    81\t\n    82\t\n    83\t;; =============================================================================\n    84\t;; Canonical request-target paths\n    85\t;; =============================================================================\n    86\t\n    87\t(def ^:private ascii-printable\n    88\t  \" !\\\"#$%&'()*+,-./0123456789:;<=>?@ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ[\\\\]^_`abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz{|}~\")\n    89\t\n    90\t\n    91\t(def ^:private unreserved\n    92\t  (set (map str (seq \"abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789-._~\"))))\n    93\t\n    94\t\n    95\t(def ^:private hex-values\n    96\t  (merge (zipmap (map str (seq \"0123456789\")) (range 10))\n    97\t         (zipmap (map str (seq \"abcdef\")) (range 10 16))\n    98\t         (zipmap (map str (seq \"ABCDEF\")) (range 10 16))))\n    99\t\n   100\t\n   940\tThe general form matters more than this instance. Every future service that\n   941\tanswers a question (a lookup, a query against a remote peer, a lease grant, a\n   942\tname resolution) faces the same three shapes. Without a rule each will choose\n   943\tby local convenience, and the convenient choice on the JVM is the one that\n   944\tdoes not port.\n   945\t\n   946\t**Proposed resolution.** One rule, stated here because this is where the\n   947\targument for it lives, and one consequence, stated in `dao.jing.md`:\n   948\t\n   949\t1. Add to *Composition*:\n   950\t\n   951\t   > The IO model is inherited. An interpreter's portable interface is\n   952\t   > expressed the way this contract's is: a step that returns what is true\n   953\t   > now, including \"not yet\", over streams the caller supplied. A waiting\n   954\t   > call or a callback may wrap that step as **host policy**, named as such\n   955\t   > and confined to the hosts that can afford it, exactly as\n   956\t   > `connect-content!` wraps the stepped client. It is never the interface\n   957\t   > another interpreter is written against.\n   958\t\n   959\t2. In `dao.jing.md`, name the stepped request/response pair as the\n   960\t   agent-facing interface to content, and reclassify the synchronous handle\n   961\t   (`:get-content-fn`) as what it already is in practice: the store\n   962\t   interpreter's boundary adapter onto its own medium, plus a cache readers may\n   963\t   consult. The handle map does not change. What changes is which of the three\n   964\t   shapes new code is allowed to depend on.\n   965\t\n   966\tTwo costs follow and should be accepted with open eyes. The tree walks in\n   967\t`dao.space.index` and `dao.data.btree.storage` become steppable: fetch, receive\n   968\t\"not yet\", return state, resume when the response is observed. Inside `yin.vm`\n   969\tthis is the existing park and wait-set path; in library code it is the shape\n   970\t`forward-step` and `rpc/poll!` already have. And `hydrate-async` is either\n   971\trewritten as a step or explicitly labelled host policy. The hydration cache\n   972\titself survives unchanged: prefetching a graph so that a walk never misses is a\n   973\tlegitimate optimization of a stepped interface, not an alternative to one.\n   974\t\n   975\t**Cost if deferred.** The nine call sites grow. Every new consumer of content\n   976\twritten on the JVM is written against the waiting shape, and each one is a\n   977\tseparate port when the first browser or Dart agent needs to read a remote\n   978\tindex.\n   979\t\n   980\t### Recorded, not proposed\n   981\t\n   982\tThese came out of the same review. Each is reachable by addition under the\n   983\trules the contract already has, so none needs a decision now. They are listed\n   984\tso that a later plan does not rediscover them as surprises.\n   985\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
